From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SheepyLord <37598021+SheepyLord@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 04:01:21 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Keep one copy of managed GMod textures and release idle
 images

Static managed 2D textures (patch 0022's class) get two memory options,
automatic for gmod.exe and off for other games:

- d3d9.releaseManagedHostCopies (DXVK_RELEASE_MANAGED_HOST_COPIES; Auto, True,
  False): once the image holds a texture's data and the texture was not locked
  for d3d9.managedHostCopyIdleSeconds (10), its CPU copy is freed. A later lock
  reads the subresource back from the GPU first, as DXVK's evictManagedOnUnlock
  path does. Auto is on below 28 GiB of RAM or 15 GiB of VRAM. Textures that
  use AddDirtyRect keep their copy.
- d3d9.managedTextureBudgetMiB (DXVK_MANAGED_TEXTURE_BUDGET_MIB; -1 automatic
  = VRAM / 4, 0 off, else MiB): while these textures hold more GPU memory than
  the budget, images not drawn for d3d9.managedTextureIdleSeconds (60 presented
  seconds) are released, least recently used first, a few per second. Their
  data stays (read back first when only the GPU had it), every subresource is
  marked for upload, and the next use recreates the image with the hash it
  had. Bound, locked and pending textures are never chosen. The released image
  is marked application-released, so Remix frees it once no instance samples
  it; ImGui drops only its own entry for that image.

The hash API and the hash-on-write rule see the kept hash while an image is
released. The resolved values are logged at startup; [RTX Managed Textures]
lines report the passes and the bytes of texture CPU copies, which the census
now names "d3d9 texture copy". Pure policy in d3d9_managed_memory.h with
test_managed_texture_memory.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
